<h2>The prosecution case</h2>

<p>The parties married in March 2000, and discord followed — on the prosecution version, over the wife\'s wish to live separately from her mother-in-law and over property-linked demands. In April 2001 the wife alleged that her husband forcibly administered Baygon insecticide to her, first from a tumbler and then from the container itself. She was taken to hospital; charges under Sections 498A and 307 IPC followed. The trial court acquitted the husband and his mother of the cruelty charge but convicted the husband of attempt to murder, sentencing him to three years\' rigorous imprisonment. His appeal remained pending in the High Court for twenty-two years.</p>

<h2>How the conviction came apart</h2>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>The victim\'s own record</h3><p>Cross-examination materially contradicted her account on dowry demands and connected allegations, eroding the credibility a forcible-poisoning narrative needs.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>The medical picture</h3><p>Mild symptoms only, normal recorded parameters, no documented indicators of serious poisoning — a clinical record at odds with insecticide forced down by an assailant.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>The forensic contradiction</h3><p>Gastric lavage negative for poison; and the container documented as empty at seizure was reported by the FSL to contain residue — an unexplained inconsistency in the chain of evidence.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>The accused\'s conduct</h3><p>He promptly arranged hospital treatment and deposited advance fees — conduct the Court expressly held relevant to, and inconsistent with, murderous intention.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Section 307 IPC (now Section 109 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023) requires the prosecution to prove an act coupled with the intention or knowledge that characterises murder. Courts routinely infer that state of mind from the weapon, the body part targeted, the severity of injury and the surrounding circumstances. This judgment adds emphasis to a further circumstance defence counsel should never overlook: what the accused did in the minutes and hours after the incident.</p>
</div>

<h2>Takeaways for practice</h2>

<p>First, in poisoning allegations the scientific trail is decisive — the lavage report, the FSL analysis of the substance, and the seizure memoranda must be aligned, and every mismatch is doubt. Second, post-incident conduct cuts both ways: flight and concealment aggravate, while immediate rescue efforts mitigate against an inference of intent; both sides should marshal this material deliberately. Third, matrimonial context calls for particular care in charge framing — the distance between a bitter quarrel with an impulsive act and a true attempt to murder is precisely the distance between Section 307 and lesser offences, and the evidence must be tested against the graver ingredient, not assumed into it.</p>

<p>Finally, the twenty-two-year life of this appeal is its own lesson. For appellants, suspension of sentence and bail pending appeal make survival of the wait possible; for the system, the case illustrates why the Supreme Court has pressed High Courts to review aged criminal appeals. An acquittal in 2026 for an incident of 2001 is justice, but justice long delayed.</p>

<p>The chamber of Advocate Manish Jha appears in criminal trials and appeals before the District Courts of Delhi, the High Court of Delhi and the Supreme Court of India. This article is for general information; it is not legal advice and does not create an advocate-client relationship.</p>
